    {
      "_id": "claim-fatiha-basmalah-ibn-kathir",
      "_type": "interpretiveClaim",
      "ayah": {
        "_type": "reference",
        "_ref": "ayah-1-1"
      },
      "source": {
        "_type": "reference",
        "_ref": "source-ibn-kathir"
      },
      "targetSegmentArabic": "بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ",
      "targetSegmentEnglish": "The Basmalah in Al-Fatiha",
      "opinionEnglish": "The Basmalah is a full, independent verse at the start of Al-Fatiha according to the Shafi'i legal school, Imam Ahmad in one prominent narration, and the reciters of Mecca and Kufa. Ibn Kathir confirms that reciting it (aloud or silently) is authentically supported by narrations from the Companions.",
      "opinionArabic": "البسملة آية مستقلة في أول سورة الفاتحة عند الشافعي وأحمد في رواية وقراء مكة والكوفة، وثبت الجهر والإسرار بها عن جماعة من الصحابة.",
      "divergenceType": "contradictory",
      "evidenceEnglish": "Transmissions in Sunan al-Daraqutni and canonical Kufan scriptural numbering counting it as verse 1."
    },
    {
      "_id": "claim-fatiha-basmalah-al-qurtubi",
      "_type": "interpretiveClaim",
      "ayah": {
        "_type": "reference",
        "_ref": "ayah-1-1"
      },
      "source": {
        "_type": "reference",
        "_ref": "source-al-qurtubi"
      },
      "targetSegmentArabic": "بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ",
      "targetSegmentEnglish": "The Basmalah in Al-Fatiha",
      "opinionEnglish": "The Basmalah is not a verse of Al-Fatiha, nor is it a verse of any chapter in the Quran except inside Surah An-Naml (27:30). According to Imam Malik and the Madinan school, it was revealed for blessing and delimiting chapters, not as a counted ayah.",
      "opinionArabic": "ليست البسملة آية من الفاتحة ولا من غيرها من السور إلا في سورة النمل. وهو المعتمد في مذهب الإمام مالك وأهل المدينة، ونزلت للتبرك والفصل بين السور.",
      "divergenceType": "contradictory",
      "evidenceEnglish": "The unbroken communal practice of Medina (Amal Ahl al-Madinah) and the authentic hadith of Aisha (RA) confirming the Prophet initiated recitation directly with 'Al-Hamdu lillahi Rabbi al-Alamin'."
    },
    {
